  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "\\sigma_\\lambda \\star \\sigma_\\mu\\ =\\ \\sum_{\\nu,\\, d \\ge 0} q^{d}\\, c_{\\lambda\\mu}^{\\nu, d}\\, \\sigma_\\nu \\quad\\text{in}\\quad QH^*(\\mathrm{Gr}(k,n)),",
    "plain": "Hilbert's 15th sought rigorous foundations for Schubert calculus — the classical count of geometric incidences on Grassmannians, governed by the Littlewood–Richardson rule. Quantum cohomology deforms the cup product by \"quantum corrections\" counting rational curves (Gromov–Witten invariants). This question asks how those corrections modify the classical intersection numbers: what is the structure of the quantum product $\\sigma_\\lambda \\star \\sigma_\\mu$ relative to the classical one?",
